  :adr/title "The shape of a five-repository split, measured before moving anything"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-07"
  :adr/related "adr-2609075000-the-passkey-is-the-wallet"
  :adr/body "## What was asked

cloud-itonami-app / -cli / -api / -apex に整理してほしい、および -auth も分離。

None of `-cli`, `-api`, `-apex`, `-auth` exists: not in `manifest/west.yml`, and
`GET /repos/cloud-itonami/<name>` is 404 for all four. So this is four new
repositories and a split, not a re-registration.

## What is being split

    src        235 files   87,064 lines
    test       236 files   56,062 lines
    resources   36 files   27,090 lines
    docs       118 files   13,633 lines
    services    33 files    6,501 lines   (5 Cloudflare Workers)
    bin         19 files    6,356 lines

with a resident on this machine running `kbb -M:server` from a release of
it, a launchd job, an MCP server, and four pointers that have to stay in step
(main, west pin, `~/.cloud-itonami/current`, the running process).

## The seams, measured

**`cloud-itonami-cli` is clean** -- but not for the reason written here
first, and the correction is the useful part.

The original claim was that `bin/itonami` reaches into `src` for exactly two
namespaces and *neither reaches back into the app*:

    commands.cljc     350 lines   requires: itself only
    repo_profile.cljc 329 lines   requires: itself only

That is true and it decides nothing. Whether a namespace can move out is
settled by who requires IT, and that had not been looked at. Measured
2026-09-07:

    cloud.itonami.app.repo-profile   bin/itonami, its own test. No app-side use.
    cloud.itonami.app.commands       bin/itonami, tests, and cli.clj.

One app-side consumer, and it sets the dependency direction between two of the
four repositories: if `cli.clj` is live, moving `commands` makes the app depend
on the CLI; leaving it makes the CLI depend on an app checkout at runtime,
which is the coupling a split is meant to remove.

`cli.clj` is neither. There is no `:cli` alias in `deps.edn` -- the file's own
comment at line 591 says a test goes red \"the day :cli is reintroduced\". It is
tested but has no launcher; PR #270 removed it and `bin/itonami` replaced it.

So the conclusion holds and the reasoning did not. Everything else the CLI
needs is its own siblings (`itonami_editor`, `itonami_splash`, `itonami_text`,
`itonami_harness`, `itonami_profile`, `itonami_skills`) and its own tests, and
this moves as a unit.

**`bin/cloud-itonami-app` is not.** It goes through `cli.clj` (881 lines),
which requires eleven app namespaces including `store` and `server-process`.
That launcher belongs with the app, not the CLI.

**`-auth` is the hard one, and the hard part is not what was written here
first.** The original entry said the question was what the app asks the auth
library FOR -- a session record, or a decision. Measured 2026-09-07, that is
not the question:

    require-session! / require-app-session! / require-human-session!
      are defined IN server.clj (line 484), not in identity. They read the
      HTTP exchange and throw ex-info. That is HTTP glue, not auth.

    The real boundary is identity/, and it is:
      64 files require it, 346 call sites in src.
      The app asks it for BOTH records and decisions, and that is fine --
      92 of those 346 are shared error vocabulary (unauthenticated,
      invalid-origin, invalid-csrf, forbidden, organization-required,
      agent-session-forbidden, invalid-registration), which is cheap to move.

What actually blocks the split is one line in identity.clj's own ns form: **it
requires `cloud.itonami.app.store`** (plus `connectors`, `credential`, `did`,
`passkey`, `session-handoff`, `org-root-did`, `email-login`, `identity-axis`).
The auth library depends on the app's persistence. So the decision is: does
auth own its own storage, or does it take a store interface? Until that is
answered, moving it makes the app depend on the auth repo AND the auth repo
depend on the app.

**A second reason arrived the same day.** ADR-2609070400 made human
authentication Web3 first for the workspace, and
`manifest/human-authentication-policy.edn` records this app as `:role :client`,
`:active-methods #{:webauthn-passkey}`, `:status :unverified`. So this surface
is about to gain a whole authentication method (SIWE + ERC-191 / ERC-1271) and
currently carries an open compliance gap. **Splitting a surface with an
unclosed gap moves the gap into a new repository without closing it.** Close
the gap, then split.

**`-apex` was separable, and is done** -- see
`adr-2609076000-the-apex-workers-leave-the-app`. Four of the five Workers
moved; `app-edge` stayed, because its dependency on `fleet-core` /
`kotoba-oracle` is in the shipped Worker rather than only in a test.

**`-api` needs a boundary that does not exist yet.** Measured 2026-09-07:
`server.clj` is 7,425 lines, 73 requires, 147 `defn` (142 private), and one
top-level form spanning **2,712 lines** starting at line 3199. There is no HTTP
layer to lift away from a domain layer -- there is one file that is both.
Creating that seam is a refactor of a live server, and it can happen inside
this repository before any repository exists. A repository does not create a
boundary; it can only hold one that is already there.

## The order this should go in

1. **`cloud-itonami-cli`** -- move `bin/itonami*`, the two `.cljc`, and their
   tests. The app keeps a dependency on the CLI's `commands` table (or the
   table moves and the app depends on it; that is one decision to make once).
2. **`cloud-itonami-apex`** -- the Workers that serve the public surface, which
   are already separate deployables with their own `wrangler` configs.
3. **`cloud-itonami-auth`** -- only after deciding what its interface is. A
   library the app calls, not a service, unless the hosted
   `auth.itonami.cloud` is meant to move here too, which is a bigger decision.
4. **`cloud-itonami-api`** -- last, because it requires splitting `server.clj`
   into routes and handlers first, which is a refactor inside the current repo
   and can happen before any repository exists.

## Status

Steps 1 and 2 are done; 3 and 4 are measured and blocked, each for a named
reason above.

Step 1 is done. `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-cli` exists, is registered in west
at pin `cb85ffd3`, and runs standalone -- `--print-data-dir` resolves to its own
tree and `itonami status` answers the live resident reading nothing from the app.
See `adr-2609075600-the-cli-leaves-the-app` in that repository.

Nothing is deleted from this repository yet. Both trees carry the CLI, both
suites pass, and `~/.local/bin/itonami` still resolves through
`~/.cloud-itonami/current`. Removing this copy repoints that launcher, so it is
a separate change and there is never a moment when nothing works.

Steps 2-4 (`-apex`, `-auth`, `-api`) are not started, for the reasons above.

The reason for writing this before moving anything: a repository split is the
kind of change that looks finished long before it is, and the thing that tells
you it is not is the deploy path -- release directory, launchd job, MCP server,
west pin -- which has to keep working at every step. Four of those pointers
were only brought into agreement today.
"}]
